title op6: a multi-name delta whose losses sit in one fragment

# The case the old machinery got WRONG, and the only one of the four
# op6 shapes whose verdict the settlement changes.  Gold is the
# settled answer; the engine is behind it until wave 2, and that is
# the point of writing the fixture now.
#
# Onto un-shares z.  Off-of renames two names, x to aa and y to bb.
# The delta is lost {x, y}, gained {aa, bb}, and both lost names lie
# in the SAME fragment -- the one onto left holding x and y.
#
# OLD BEHAVIOUR.  Two lost and two gained fell to the degrade branch,
# the gains became attachments, the attachment links met the split's
# exclusions, and pass 2 reported a pooling conflict.  The two sides
# had not disagreed about anything: off-of renamed two names, onto
# un-shared a third, and no name was claimed twice.  The conflict was
# a statement about what the algorithm could not do.
#
# SETTLED BEHAVIOUR.  There is nothing to pair.  The delta routes
# WHOLESALE to the fragment holding its lost names, which is
# determined precisely because the lost set does not straddle the
# split.  Realization pass one finds no decided name in onto -- aa
# and bb are both new -- and pass two takes the lost-set route to
# pool 10, which the z fragment has not claimed and whose type
# matches.  Clean, and the fragment keeps its dnode.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none; no directory changed
#   deltas:          pool 10 lost {x, y}, gained {aa, bb}; the lost
#                    set lies wholly inside onto's fragment 10-100
#   decisions:       output pool {aa, bb} reuses onto's 10-100 by the
#                    lost-set route; output pool {z} reuses 30-200 by
#                    its surviving name.  No conflict, no quarantine
#   conflicts:       none.  Under the OLD rule: conflict pooling,
#                    with /shared/x, /shared/y and /shared/z
#                    quarantined and the region falling back to onto
#   emission:        two renames inside /shared's scope
#
# So this fixture is expected to FAIL against the current engine, and
# failing is what it is for.  When wave 2 lands set-valued
# succession, it turns green and that is the evidence the change
# arrived.  Of the four op6 shapes it is the only one that can carry
# that evidence: two decide identically before and after, and the
# fourth conflicts both ways.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y
      10-100: z

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y
      30-200: z = v0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: aa = v0
      10-100: bb
      10-100: z

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared
      10-100: aa = v0
      10-100: bb
      30-200: z = v0
